    "result_text": "I've implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py` and all 7 tests pass (`pytest -q`), including 5 new ones.\n\n**How it works:** it runs `iter_answer` and joins the pieces into one string, retrying up to `ATTEMPTS` times. Each attempt starts with an empty buffer, so text from a dropped attempt is never returned.\n\n**What I checked in openai 3.19.2:**\n- The SDK wraps network errors both when connecting and while streaming (`_streaming.py:57-60`). The error you get is `APIConnectionError` or `APITimeoutError`, and the original error is attached as its `__cause__`.\n- `APITimeoutError` is a subclass of `APIConnectionError`, so the timeout case has to be caught first. Otherwise a stalled stream would be retried.\n- The SDK's own message is always just \"Connection error.\", which wouldn't tell ops anything. The WARNING logs the original error instead, e.g. `Attempt 2/3 to reach the model failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body (incomplete chunked read)`.\n\n**Behaviour:**\n- **Connection fails or drops mid-stream:** logs a WARNING on `app.chat` and retries from scratch.\n- **Every attempt fails:** raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, chained to the last error.\n- **Timeout, whether while connecting or mid-stream:** raises `UpstreamTimeout` straight away with no retry.\n- The client is used as given. Its own `max_retries` still applies inside each attempt.\n\n**Tests:** the new ones in `tests/test_chat.py` use a real `openai.OpenAI` client with a fake network layer (`httpx2.MockTransport`, already installed), so they check how the SDK actually reports these errors. They cover:\n- a normal answer\n- a refused connection, then a mid-stream drop, then success (with checks on the two log messages and that no partial text comes back)\n- all attempts failing\n- a timeout while connecting, and a stall mid-stream, neither of which is retried\n\nNo dependencies were added."
